Exception for the historical reference data:
The Wikipedia-derived Fontenoy catalogue and approach timeline are distributed
under Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International:
https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/
The application includes their contributor attribution, source revision links
and description of adaptations in Historical OOB > Sources and unresolved
questions. Repository references are recorded in docs/HISTORICAL_OOB_SOURCES.md.
This exception applies to those data adaptations, not to application code.

Exception for the bundled Crimean War map scans:
The two historical maps in assets/maps/crimea are public-domain works and are
not covered by the application-code rights reservation above.

1. James Wyld, The Crimea (London, 1 May 1855).
   Library of Congress, Geography and Map Division; item 2015591080.
   https://www.loc.gov/item/2015591080/
   Digital copy obtained via Wikimedia Commons:
   https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Crimea_LOC_2015591080.jpg
   LOC states its Geography and Map digitized collection is free to use and
   reuse unless an item-specific Rights Advisory indicates otherwise.

2. Military sketch of the fortress of Sevastopol and adjacent country,
   from Richard Airey's Opening address (1856), British Library HMNTS 9077.e.15.
   British Library Mechanical Curator collection, via Wikimedia Commons:
   https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fortress_of_SEVASTOPOL_and_adjacent_country.jpg
   The file is identified as public domain, including pre-1931 US publication.
   The source notes scan enhancement for machine interpretation. The app adds
   live overlays separately; historical troop positions remain printed in the scan.

Source metadata, download URLs, dimensions and checksums are recorded in
assets/maps/crimea/SOURCES.md. These notices do not license Maurice tactical
rules or alter the licensing of the application code.
